# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Church Dashboard — bootstrap environment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This file is INTENTIONALLY SHORT. Anything an operator might want to change
# at runtime — site name, SMTP, Google / Microsoft OAuth, UniFi, ProPresenter,
# Planning Center, auth feature flags, etc. — is stored in the `settings`
# database table and edited at /admin/settings in the browser.
#
# Only put things here that have to exist BEFORE the containers start, or
# that wire one container to another:
#   - host port mappings           (operator-tunable)
#   - cryptographic secrets        (must be set before first boot)
#   - service connection strings   (in-stack addresses + credentials)
#
# If you find yourself adding an entry here for a *feature* setting, stop —
# add it to KNOWN_SETTINGS in packages/shared/src/settings.ts instead.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- Host ports (operator-tunable) ----
# These are the only ports actually bound on the host. Change if any conflict
# with other projects on the same machine. `make check-ports` warns about
# collisions before `make up`.
EXTERNAL_PORT=8100         # public proxy (the only port real users need)
COCKROACH_UI_PORT=8180     # dev, DB_MODE=bundled only: Cockroach admin UI
# Dev, DB_MODE=bundled only: where Cockroach writes its logs (default
# ./data/cockroach-logs). Point it at local disk if ./data is on NFS.
# COCKROACH_LOG_DIR=/var/lib/church-cockroach-logs

# ---- Runtime ----
NODE_ENV=development
# LOG_LEVEL is bootstrap-only because the logger is wired up before the DB
# is reachable. trace | debug | info | warn | error.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---- Auth ----
# AUTH_SECRET is the master secret, shared identically by the web + api
# containers. It does four things:
#   1. signs/encrypts the Auth.js session cookie (web)
#   2. lets the api validate that cookie on every request + WebSocket
#   3. derives the web->api internal-call token (sha256 of it)
#   4. encrypts secret settings (SMTP password, OAuth secrets) at rest in the DB
# It must exist before first boot (it protects the DB's own secrets, so it can't
# live in the DB). Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# WARNING: changing it logs everyone out AND makes encrypted settings unreadable
# — you'd have to re-enter SMTP/OAuth secrets at /admin/settings. Back up this
# file together with ./data (a data-only backup cannot be decrypted).
AUTH_SECRET=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-hex-32
# APP_URL / AUTH_URL: intentionally unset. Auth.js (trustHost: true) derives
# the public origin from the incoming Host header so the app works at every
# hostname you point at it (localhost, LAN IP, *.example.com, an HTTPS LB,
# whatever) with zero config. Only set these if a reverse proxy strips/
# rewrites *both* Host AND X-Forwarded-Host.

# ---- Database ----
# DB_MODE picks where the database lives (scripts/compose.sh reads it):
#   bundled   CockroachDB runs inside this stack (single node in dev, 3 in
#             prod). DATABASE_URL is set for you; leave it unset.
#   external  a database you already run: YugabyteDB (YSQL, port 5433) or
#             CockroachDB (port 26257). Set DATABASE_URL; the containers must
#             be able to reach it. The engine is detected automatically.
# The database named in the URL must exist; migrations create the tables. On
# YugabyteDB 2024.2 (PG 11 based) migrations enable pgcrypto for
# gen_random_uuid(), which needs a user allowed to CREATE EXTENSION (or have
# the DBA run: CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgcrypto;).
DB_MODE=bundled
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://church:secret@yb-host.example.org:5433/church?sslmode=require
# A database that is a cluster: list EVERY node, comma separated, and the app connects to whichever
# answers, spreads its connections over them and moves to another when one fails: no load balancer
# between the app and the database. Special characters in the user or password are %-encoded.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://church:secret@yb1.example.org:5433,yb2.example.org:5433,yb3.example.org:5433/church?sslmode=require
# DB_QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS: how long a statement may go unanswered before its connection is dropped and the
# statement retried on another (default 60000; 0 = never). It is what notices a database host that hangs
# or loses power without closing its connections. Raise it only if you run statements longer than that.
# DB_QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# ---- Behind a load balancer / several nodes (docs/multi-node.md) ----
# TRUSTED_PROXIES: space-separated CIDRs of the load balancer(s) in front of this node.
# Set it so the client IP in the audit log and the rate limits is the real one and cannot
# be forged with an X-Forwarded-For header. Unset trusts every peer (and takes the first
# entry of X-Forwarded-For), which is only safe if something upstream overwrites that header.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.0.0.0/24
# BUILD_ID names the build (default: from git, see scripts/build-id.sh). Every node must run
# the same build: build once with scripts/release.sh, or build the same clean commit on each.
# API_IMAGE / WEB_IMAGE / MONITOR_IMAGE run pre-built images instead of building (release.sh
# prints these lines).
# BUILD_ID=
# API_IMAGE=
# WEB_IMAGE=
# MONITOR_IMAGE=

# ---- This node (docs/multi-node.md) ----
# Only matters once more than one app node shares a database; a single node
# can leave all of these alone.
# NODE_ID is this node's name: stable across restarts, unique per node.
# NODE_ID=main
# BACKGROUND_JOBS=off keeps this node from ever taking a periodic job (polling,
# schedulers), for a node that cannot reach the monitored devices. Default on.
# BACKGROUND_JOBS=on

# ---- A cluster of nodes with a bundled database (shape C; INSTALL.md) ----
# DEPLOY_MODE=cluster runs this node as one of several (production stack only). Then every
# node needs the lines below, different where marked. The nodes' databases form one cluster
# (secure mode, certificates from scripts/cluster.sh init-certs) and, with S3_MODE=bundled,
# so do their object stores.
# DEPLOY_MODE=single
# NODE_ID=node-a                    # DIFFERENT on every node
# NODE_ADDR=10.0.0.11               # DIFFERENT: how the other nodes reach this one (IP or DNS name)
# CLUSTER_PEERS=10.0.0.12,10.0.0.13 # DIFFERENT: the OTHER nodes' NODE_ADDRs, comma separated
# CLUSTER_BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0         # host interface the cluster ports are published on; firewall
#                                   # them to the other nodes only
# CLUSTER_DB_PORT=26257             # database port (same on every node unless several share a host)
# CLUSTER_S3_RPC_PORT=3901          # object store RPC port (likewise)
# CLUSTER_S3_CAPACITY=100G          # what each node offers the object store; a ceiling, not allocated
# CLUSTER_S3_API_PORT=              # optional: publish this node's S3 API (to copy files in or out; firewall it, unset after)
# NODE_ROLE=full                    # `data` = a witness that runs only the database and object store
# GARAGE_REPLICATION_FACTOR=        # set when the cluster is created; default 3 with 3+ nodes, else 2
# With a remote database and a remote object store (DB_MODE=external, S3_MODE=external) a
# cluster needs only DEPLOY_MODE and NODE_ID of these: nodes find each other through the database.

# ---- Object storage (uploaded files: wiki attachments, note images, ...) ----
# S3_MODE picks where they live (scripts/compose.sh reads it):
#   bundled   the Garage in this stack (an S3-compatible store). Nothing to configure: its
#             access key and internal secrets are derived from AUTH_SECRET, so every node of
#             a cluster (which shares AUTH_SECRET) gets the same ones.
#   external  an S3-compatible store you run or rent (AWS S3, Backblaze B2, your own Garage
#             or MinIO): set the S3_* lines. The bucket must exist, unless the credentials
#             may create it. The bundled Garage is not started.
S3_MODE=bundled
# S3_BUCKET=church-files
#
# Bundled Garage only: where its metadata database lives. It is an LMDB database and MUST be
# on local disk, never NFS/SMB (the container refuses to start otherwise). The default is
# under ./data; if ./data is a network share, point this at a local folder.
# GARAGE_META_DIR=/var/lib/church-garage-meta
#
# For S3_MODE=external:
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.example.org     # host[:port] or a URL, no path; https:// turns TLS on
# S3_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_SECRET_KEY=
# S3_REGION=us-east-1                    # a Garage store is "garage"
# S3_SESSION_TOKEN=                      # only for temporary credentials
# S3_USE_SSL=true                        # only needed if S3_ENDPOINT has no https://
# S3_PATH_STYLE=true                     # host/bucket/key (Garage, MinIO, most self-hosted);
#                                        # false for virtual-hosted bucket.host/key (AWS S3)
# A store with a private CA: give the containers the CA with NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS.
#
# The older MINIO_* names (MINIO_ENDPOINT, MINIO_REGION, MINIO_BUCKET, MINIO_USE_SSL, and
# MINIO_ROOT_USER / MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD as the keys) are still read for an external store when
# the S3_* one is not set. The bundled store used to be MinIO; see INSTALL.md to move an
# existing install over.

# ---- Meilisearch ----
MEILI_URL=http://meilisearch:7700
MEILI_MASTER_KEY=dev-master-key-change-in-prod

# ---- Internal service URLs (Docker network) ----
# Don't change unless you're rewiring the compose stack. These are how the
# web container reaches the api and vice versa over the internal bridge.
API_INTERNAL_URL=http://api:3001
WEB_INTERNAL_URL=http://web:3000
